    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHY THIS FACT EXISTS. `nat_axiom_inventory` reporting `nat: axiom=0` says nothing about whether the object is the natural numbers: an inductive type named `Nat` with a subtly wrong order would report the same zero and typecheck the same modules. Rendered Lean modules make the gap concrete -- they run in `prelude` mode and RE-DECLARE their own `Nat`, `Eq` and `False`, so official Lean accepting one certifies `this proof typechecks against THESE definitions`, not that they are the standard ones. This fact closes that by proof rather than by inspection, and it is stronger than a bridge lemma to one other definition of the naturals because it quantifies over every structure satisfying the axioms. NOT VACUOUS: `characterization_tests::categoricity_is_not_vacuous_it_instantiates_at_nat_itself` instantiates the theorem at (Nat, zero, succ) with the three Peano theorems as its hypotheses and pushes the result back through the trusted gate -- a categoricity theorem whose premises nothing satisfies would be axiom-free and worthless. LOAD-BEARING: six injected `Weakening` variants replace one hypothesis of these theorems with `True` and the kernel must refuse each ONE AT THE DECLARATION IT WAS AIMED AT; a guard-mutation check (disabling one injection) killed exactly one test.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
